      "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
      "body": ">  This is a badly-encoded signature because there is an overflowing s value\n\nThe number is properly DER-encoded. It just happens to be out of bounds. But being in bounds is a requirement from ECDSA spec, not from the DER spec.\n\n> Maybe this is a non-goal of the API (or maybe it's a goal but nobody wants to touch consensus code to fix it), but it feels weird to me that the existing parser sometimes \"parses\" a signature into something that doesn't resemble the original signature. At the very least we should document this.\n\nWhat's wrong with the current docs?\n\nhttps://github.com/bitcoin-core/secp256k1/blob/307035b302eb7e9b5325638a377e64e6b29c41a3/include/secp256k1.h#L555-L562\n\nThe way I see it is this API guarantee makes a lot of sense. It keeps parsing and signature verification properly separated. What I'd ideally except from a parsing function is that it accepts *any* properly encoded signature. `secp256k1_ecdsa_signature_parse_der` does precisely this. DER can encode arbitrarily large integers, so it's entirely reasonable for `secp256k1_ecdsa_signature_parse_der` to accept arbitrarily large integers. Whether they're out of range (or what this even means) is a separate issue and belongs to signature verification.\n\nThe fact that the out-of-range values are internally set to 0 is an implementation detail. An API caller can't observe this.\n\n> This means that a user trying to call `parse_der` then \"falling back\" to `parse_der_lax` will reject the signature.\n\nI think if we want to do anything here, then the docs of `parse_der_lax` could be improved. The behavior is documented but I agree that the implications, in particular when compared to `parse_der`, are not obvious:\n\nhttps://github.com/bitcoin-core/secp256k1/blob/307035b302eb7e9b5325638a377e64e6b29c41a3/contrib/lax_der_parsing.h#L32-L35\n\n(But see also https://github.com/bitcoin-core/secp256k1/issues/758)\n\n> More generally, the API provides no way for users to determine whether a signature that fails to validate failed because it's actually a bad sig,,or becasue it violates the BIP66 rules.\n\nIf I'm not mistaken the API precisely offers this way. BIP66 mandates strict DER encoding, precisely what `secp256k1_ecdsa_signature_parse_der` gives you.\n \n---\n\nAs a side note, our Schnorr signature API avoids this separation issue entirely by just having a single function that does both parsing and verification. And that's most convenient and safest API: A (well-designed) caller should only care about whether a given signature is valid or invalid. Why it's invalid shouldn't matter, and indicating it to the caller only pushes more complexity in terms of reasoning and error handling to the caller.\n\nHowever, Bitcoin violates this design rule when it comes to ECDSA, so we need to offer two separate functions. Quoting BIP 66:\n\n> [...] if the signature does not pass the IsValidSignatureEncoding check below, the entire script evaluates to false immediately. If the signature is valid DER, but does not pass ECDSA verification, opcode execution continues as it used to [...]\n",
