      "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
      "body": "This is documented, though not clearly enough for the fallback case. `include/secp256k1.h` says `parse_der` \"will accept any valid DER encoded signature, even if the encoded numbers are out of range\", and that verification with such a signature then always fails. The `s` here is not an overflow: its top bit is set with no 0x00 pad, so it is valid DER for a negative integer. `parse_der` treats it like an `s` >= n and returns 1 with s = 0. That was the design when the parser was rewritten in 3bb9c44: \"the data has to be exact DER, though the values inside don't need to be valid\".\n\nWhat the header does not say is that a negative R or s counts as out of range, and that a caller who tries `parse_der` first never reaches the lax fallback for such a signature. One sentence saying both would cover your case. Returning 0 for a negative integer would also allow the fallback, but it changes that documented behaviour.\n\nFor comparison, the strict parsers I tried (btcec `ParseDERSignature`, python-ecdsa, cryptography, bitcoinjs-lib's bip66) reject this signature. The lax ones (Core's `ecdsa_signature_parse_der_lax`, btcec `ParseSignature`) read s as unsigned, and it verifies. I can share the harness if useful.\n",
